  "notes": "{悼|いた}む is used specifically for mourning the dead. It is more formal and literary than {悲|かな}しむ.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: {他動詞|たどうし} (transitive)\n- Pattern: [person/death]を{悼|いた}む\n\nGodan verb ({五段動詞|ごだんどうし}), む-ending.\n- Negative: {悼|いた}まない\n- Te-form: {悼|いた}んで\n- Past: {悼|いた}んだ\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {死|し}を{悼|いた}む: to mourn someone's death\n- {故人|こじん}を{悼|いた}む: to mourn the deceased\n- {犠牲者|ぎせいしゃ}を{悼|いた}む: to mourn victims\n- {哀悼|あいとう}の{意|い}を{表|あらわ}す: to express condolences (related formal expression)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {悼|いた}む: to mourn (specifically for the dead, formal)\n- {悲|かな}しむ: to feel sad, to grieve (broader, any sadness)\n- {嘆|なげ}く: to lament, to grieve (includes expression of regret)\n\nUSAGE:\nDo not confuse with the homophones {痛|いた}む (to hurt, to ache) or {傷|いた}む (to be damaged).",
